MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=1282
We have the decay:  
π⁺ → μ⁺ + ν  
π⁺ at rest, so initial momentum = 0.  
Masses:  
m_π = 139.6 MeV/c²  
m_μ = 105.7 MeV/c²  
Neutrino mass ≈ 0.

Energy conservation:  
m_π c² = E_μ + E_ν  
Momentum conservation:  
p_μ = p_ν (magnitudes equal, opposite directions).

For muon: E_μ² = (p c)² + (m_μ c²)²  
For neutrino: E_ν = p c (since massless).

Let p be magnitude of momentum (×c). Then:  
E_μ = √(p² + m_μ²)  
E_ν = p  
m_π = √(p² + m_μ²) + p

Solve for p:  
m_π - p = √(p² + m_μ²)  
Square:  
(m_π - p)² = p² + m_μ²  
m_π² - 2 m_π p + p² = p² + m_μ²  
m_π² - 2 m_π p = m_μ²  
2 m_π p = m_π² - m_μ²  
p = (m_π² - m_μ²) / (2 m_π)

Compute numerically:  
m_π² = (139.6)² = 19488.16  
m_μ² = (105.7)² = 11172.49  
Difference = 19488.16 - 11172.49 = 8315.67  
Divide by 2 m_π = 2 × 139.6 = 279.2  
p = 8315.67 / 279.2 ≈ 29.78 MeV/c

Then:  
E_ν = p = 29.78 MeV (kinetic energy of neutrino, since massless).  
E_μ = √(p² + m_μ²) = √(29.78² + 105.7²)  
29.78² ≈ 886.85  
105.7² = 11172.49  
Sum = 12059.34  
√ ≈ 109.81 MeV  
KE_μ = E_μ - m_μ = 109.81 - 105.7 = 4.11 MeV.

So KE: muon ≈ 4.11 MeV, neutrino ≈ 29.78 MeV.  
Closest choice: B (4.12 MeV, 29.8 MeV).

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

