We need to compute throughput for different window sizes.  
Given:  
- Frame length = 512 bytes = 4096 bits  
- Data rate = 64 kb/s  
- Propagation delay = 270 ms  
- Acknowledgment is very short (negligible transmission time).  

First, transmission time for one frame:  
\( t_{tx} = \frac{4096 \text{ bits}}{64{,}000 \text{ b/s}} = 0.064 \text{ s} = 64 \text{ ms} \)  

Round-trip time (RTT) = 2 × propagation delay = 540 ms.  

For stop-and-wait (window size = 1):  
Throughput = \(\frac{\text{frame size}}{\text{total cycle time}}\)  
Total cycle time = \( t_{tx} + \text{RTT} = 64 + 540 = 604 \text{ ms} \)  
Throughput = \(\frac{4096}{0.604} \approx 6781.5 \text{ b/s} \approx 6.8 \text{ kb/s}\)  

For window size \(W\), throughput = \(\frac{W \times \text{frame size}}{t_{tx} + \text{RTT}}\) if \(W\) is small enough that the sender is not always transmitting. But we must check if the window fills the pipe.  

Bandwidth-delay product:  
Data rate × RTT = 64 kb/s × 0.54 s = 34.56 kb = 34,560 bits.  
Frame size = 4096 bits.  
Number of frames to fill pipe = \(\frac{34,560}{4096} \approx 8.44\). So window sizes 1, 7 are below pipe capacity; 17 and 117 are above.  

For W=7:  
Throughput = \(\frac{7 \times 4096}{0.604} \approx \frac{28672}{0.604} \approx 47470 \text{ b/s} \approx 47.5 \text{ kb/s}\)  

For W=17 and W=117, the sender transmits continuously at 64 kb/s (since window > pipe capacity). So throughput = 64 kb/s.  

Thus results:  
W=1: 6.8 kb/s  
W=7: 47.5 kb/s  
W=17: 64 kb/s  
W=117: 64 kb/s  

This matches option C.  

Answer: C
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

