ω₁: x²+y²=36. EF ⟂ BC, BC horizontal, so EF vertical. Rectangle sides vertical/horizontal. So EFGH has vertical sides EF and GH, horizontal sides FG and EH. Inscribed: vertices on circle.

Let rectangle width = 2a (horizontal), height = 2b (vertical). Center at A=(0,0) for symmetry? Not necessarily, but rectangle inscribed in circle centered at origin with sides vertical/horizontal means center is at origin. Because symmetry: if rectangle with vertical/horizontal sides is inscribed in circle centered at origin, its center must be origin. So vertices: (±a, ±b). Which labeling? Diagram: E=(5,-3.3166), F=(5,3.3166), G=(-5,3.3166), H=(-5,-3.3166). So a=5, b≈3.3166. Indeed a²+b²=36 → 25+11=36, b=√11 ≈ 3.3166. So rectangle is symmetric about origin.

