**Step 4 – Rectangle not necessarily centered**

If rectangle EFGH inscribed in ω₁ with sides vertical/horizontal, but not necessarily centered at origin, then vertices are (x₀±a, y₀±b) with (x₀,y₀) center. Condition: all four points on circle: (x₀±a)² + (y₀±b)² = 36. This gives two equations: (x₀+a)²+(y₀+b)²=36 and (x₀-a)²+(y₀+b)²=36. Subtracting gives (x₀+a)² - (x₀-a)² = 0 → 4ax₀=0 → x₀=0. Similarly from top/bottom: (x₀)²+(y₀+b)² = (x₀)²+(y₀-b)² → 4by₀=0 → y₀=0. So indeed center must be origin. So rectangle is centered. So b must satisfy a²+b²=36.

Our area equality gave b=2.5, but then a²=36-6.25=29.75, a=√119/2 ≈ 5.45, not 5. So diagram’s a=5, b=√11≈3.3166 doesn’t satisfy area equality. So we must solve correctly.

