Instead of solving messy quartic, note symmetry: Rotating parabola by \(60^\circ\) and intersecting with original: The intersection points are fixed points of the composition of the rotation and reflection? Actually, the intersection of a curve and its rotated copy often has symmetry: The intersection points lie on the rotation axis bisector?  

The rotation is \(60^\circ\). The intersection points are symmetric with respect to the rotation: If \(P\) is intersection, then rotating \(P\) by \(60^\circ\) gives another intersection point. So the set is closed under \(60^\circ\) rotation. The origin is not on parabola (\(0^2-4=-4\)).  

