QUESTION_PROMPT_BEGIN bytes=621
Assuming the signal $x(t)=(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t})^2$ has a Fourier transform result of $X(j\omega)$, with a sampling frequency $\omega_s=150\pi$, the sampled signal is $g(t)$, and the Fourier transform of $g(t)$ is $G(j\omega)$. If $G(j\omega)$ is required to satisfy: $G(j\omega)=75X(j\omega) \quad for \quad |\omega|\leq\omega_0$, what is the maximum value of $\omega_0$?

Choices:
A. 50\pi
B. 37.5\pi
C. 150\pi
D. 125\pi
E. 43.75\pi
F. 62.5\pi
G. 75\pi
H. 25\pi
I. 87.5\pi
J. 100\pi

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=1638
We have  
\[
x(t) = \left( \frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t} \right)^2
\]  
The Fourier transform of \(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}\) is a rectangle of height 1 from \(-50\pi\) to \(50\pi\). Squaring in time corresponds to convolution in frequency:  
\[
X(j\omega) = \text{triangular shape}, \text{ nonzero from } -100\pi \text{ to } 100\pi, \text{ peak } 50\pi \text{ at } \omega=0.
\]  
Specifically, the convolution of two rectangles of width \(100\pi\) gives a triangle with base from \(-100\pi\) to \(100\pi\) and maximum height \(50\pi\) (since area under each rectangle is \(100\pi\), convolution at zero gives \(100\pi\), but scaling from FT of squared sinc yields \(50\pi\) at zero).  

Sampling frequency \(\omega_s = 150\pi\) rad/s, so sampling period \(T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega_s} = \frac{2\pi}{150\pi} = \frac{1}{75}\) seconds.  

The sampled signal \(g(t) = x(t) \cdot \sum \delta(t - nT)\) has Fourier transform  
\[
G(j\omega) = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - k\omega_s)).
\]  
Here \(\frac{1}{T} = 75\). So  
\[
G(j\omega) = 75 \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - k \cdot 150\pi)).
\]  

We want \(G(j\omega) = 75 X(j\omega)\) for \(|\omega| \leq \omega_0\). This means no aliasing in that band. The baseband copy \(75 X(j\omega)\) extends from \(-100\pi\) to \(100\pi\). The nearest replicas are centered at \(\pm 150\pi\), so their edges start at \(150\pi - 100\pi = 50\pi\) and \(-150\pi + 100\pi = -50\pi\).  

Thus the maximum \(\omega_0\) without overlap is the point where the baseband triangle meets the first replica: at \(\omega = 50\pi\). So  
\[
\omega_0 = 50\pi.
\]  

Final answer:  
Answer: A
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

