We are given:

(1) \( 2Fe(s) + \frac{3}{2}O_2(g) \rightarrow Fe_2O_3(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -177\) kcal  
(2) \( 4Fe_2O_3(s) + Fe(s) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -19\) kcal  

We need the Gibbs free energy of formation of \(Fe_3O_4\) (per mole).  
The formation reaction is:  
\( 3Fe(s) + 2O_2(g) \rightarrow Fe_3O_4(s) \)

We can combine reactions to get this.

Let \(\Delta G_f(Fe_2O_3) = -177\) kcal for 1 mole of \(Fe_2O_3\)? Wait careful: Reaction (1) produces 1 mole \(Fe_2O_3\) from 2 Fe and 1.5 O₂, so \(\Delta G_f(Fe_2O_3) = -177\) kcal/mol.

Now reaction (2): \(4Fe_2O_3 + Fe \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4\), \(\Delta G = -19\) kcal for that reaction.

We want \(\Delta G_f(Fe_3O_4)\). Write formation of 3 moles \(Fe_3O_4\) from elements:

\( 3Fe + 2O_2 \rightarrow Fe_3O_4 \) (for 1 mole, but we can scale).

We can get it by:

Take 4 × reaction (1): \(8Fe + 6O_2 \rightarrow 4Fe_2O_3\), \(\Delta G = 4 \times (-177) = -708\) kcal.  
Add reaction (2): \(4Fe_2O_3 + Fe \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4\), \(\Delta G = -19\) kcal.  
Sum: \(8Fe + Fe + 6O_2 \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4\) → \(9Fe + 6O_2 \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4\), \(\Delta G = -708 -19 = -727\) kcal.

That is for 3 moles \(Fe_3O_4\). Per mole: \(-727/3 \approx -242.333...\) kcal/mol.

That matches choice D: \(-242.3\) kcal/mol (rounded).

Answer: D
