Consider an oscillating charge distribution, which is spheroid in shape (with the symmetry axis along z-axis). Because it oscillates, it would radiate at a wavelength \lambda. In the radiation zone, the radiated power per unit solid angle will be a function f of \lambda and the angle \theta (with z-axis), along which the power is measured. If the maximum power thus radiated is A, the fraction of A that will be radiated at an angle \theta = 30^0 and a possible form of f are, respectively,

Choices:
A. 1/4, \lambda^(-4)
B. 3/4, \lambda^(-6)
C. 1/2, \lambda^(-4)
D. 1/4, \lambda^(-3)

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=867
We need to determine the angular dependence and wavelength scaling for radiation from an oscillating spheroidal charge distribution.  
For a radiating source that is not a simple dipole, the angular distribution can vary.  
If the spheroid oscillates along its symmetry axis (z-axis), the lowest-order radiation is likely electric dipole radiation, which has a \(\sin^2\theta\) angular dependence.  
The power per unit solid angle for dipole radiation is proportional to \(\sin^2\theta\).  
At \(\theta = 30^\circ\), \(\sin^2 30^\circ = (1/2)^2 = 1/4\).  
Thus the fraction of maximum power (which occurs at \(\theta=90^\circ\)) is \(1/4\).  

For the wavelength dependence: dipole radiation power scales as \(\lambda^{-4}\) (from \(P \propto \omega^4\) and \(\omega \propto 1/\lambda\)).  

Thus the fraction is \(1/4\) and the form is \(\lambda^{-4}\).  

Answer: A
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

